i know that there are a ton of uh crypto  enthusiasts traders  investors holders in the david pakman  show  audience things have been particularly  crazy lately especially since  tesla uh it was revealed tesla had  purchased 1.5 billion dollars worth of  bitcoin and plans to start  accepting it and today we are going to  be talking about  a crypto trading platform called unimex  unimex  actually sponsored this video and unimex  is an on-chain margin trading platform  entirely contained within uni swap  which means that the shorts and the  longs are all executed  on uniswap rather than through an  off-chain  approach now many of you may know that  uniswap  is the largest decentralized crypto  exchange  in the world based on the ethereum  blockchain  so unimex is a decentralized finance  protocol  and they facilitate the margin trading  of native  uniswap tokens before unimex  there was no direct way to shorter long  these uniswap tokens despite  the hundreds of millions of dollars of  aggregated daily trading volumes that  uniswap attracts and the two billion  dollar  liquidity pooled in the protocol so  this is the problem that unimex is  trying to solve and within the platform  a central factory smart contract  deploys lending pool contracts which are  smart contracts  that permit the lending of specific  erc20 tokens so  what this allows is for margin traders  to borrow them  for leveraged trading unimex only  permits the correct creation of lending  pools for tokens that are trading  within an er 20 eth uniswap  pair and in unimex a lender creates a  lending pool automatically  by calling a function on the unimex  factory  which checks that the erc20 eth pair  is actually trading on uniswap by  querying the factory  while also ensuring that the calendar  seeds the sufficient  number of the erc20 tokens to the  lending pool now  there's a restriction as to which erc20  tokens are eligible for the creation of  a lending pool  subject to the liquidity of the uniswap  trading pair unfortunately a measure of  decentralization has to be sacrificed  here  but unimex says that it's simply  necessary  to combat how easily these low liquidity  pools  can be manipulated with on-chain oracles  so central to the function of the unimex  protocol  the effect of permitting free reign on  the integration of uniswap pairs of  lower  dubious liquidity could be catastrophic  imagine pairs from fraudulent projects  likely to have most of their liquidity  pulled by the project  itself but in the medium term unimec  says  they will liberate the process of  approving the creation of lending pools  from the governance of an administrator  by subjecting the decision  to a vote which users will be able to  contribute to  by burning or staking governance tokens  all  updates to the unimex platform are  regulated  by the governance using voting via the  umx token which is the unimex  ecosystem's native token as well and all  collateral  from borrows will be denominated in  ether  although this does sacrifice some  flexibility they see this as a necessary  measure to ensure control of the margin  trading process  as every asset valuation within the  protocol  will be denominated in ether and then  finally  lenders receive fees in the denomination  of the asset borrow so  ether borrows will yield ether fees  erc20 borrows will yield erc20 fees  and so on distributed among borrowers  proportional to their stakes  in the respective lending pools unimex  imposes a 0.8 levy  on all loans when margin traders pay the  fees the vast majority of the fees are  then dispersed to lenders  as a reward for supplying the liquidity  to the lending pools  and then a tiny percentage of the fees  go to umx token  holders and a fraction of a percentage  of the fees  go to the unimex team the liquidation  reserve  and to token buybacks and token burns  so it's a pretty interesting trading  platform one that's aiming to solve an  actual problem  trying to tap into a lot of u